[finding] f-020 At 7.1 keV the DW verdict depends on the normalization when compared with the XRISM limit sin^2 2theta < ~4e-10 (f-012). The server line (2.55e-10) survives XRI
**Finding:** At 7.1 keV the DW verdict depends on the normalization when compared with the XRISM limit sin^2 2theta < ~4e-10 (f-012). The server line (2.55e-10) survives XRISM. The brief's line (7.65e-10) and the published Abazajian line (3.0e-9, about +10%/-7% from T_QCD) are excluded, the published one by about 7.5x. Since the published normalization is the reference calculation, DW dark matter at 7.1 keV is excluded by XRISM alone. The server's DW Omega_s h^2 underestimates the mixing needed by about 12x at 7.1 keV, so server-based DW statements are not conservative.
[tool] tc-261021_1-w2-d-06-103 -> ok: Recorded finding f-020.
[tool] d-06 post_finding {"claim": "On the DW DM line, the standard DW-to-thermal mapping m_s = 4.43 keV (m_th/keV)^(4/3) (Viel et al. 2005, astro-ph/0501562; text downloaded, but the f
[finding] f-021 On the DW DM line, the standard DW-to-thermal mapping m_s = 4.43 keV (m_th/keV)^(4/3) (Viel et al. 2005, astro-ph/0501562; text downloaded, but the formula was 
**Finding:** On the DW DM line, the standard DW-to-thermal mapping m_s = 4.43 keV (m_th/keV)^(4/3) (Viel et al. 2005, astro-ph/0501562; text downloaded, but the formula was not quoted verbatim from it) gives m_th = 0.75 keV (m_s = 3 keV), 1.42 keV (7.1 keV), 1.84 keV (10 keV), 3.10 keV (20 keV) and 6.16 keV (50 keV). Lyman-alpha m_th > 4 (6) keV requires m_s > 28 (48) keV, and satellite counts m_th > 8.4 keV require m_s > 76 keV. At m_s >= 28 keV the DW line has sin^2 2theta ~ 3e-10 (published normalization) or about 1e-11 (server). Both values lie far above the NuSTAR ceiling of about 1e-12 at 6-40 keV and the SPI ceiling of about 3e-14 above 40 keV (f-002). The surviving DW mass interval is therefore empty under all three normalizations.
[tool] tc-261021_1-w2-d-06-104 -> ok: Recorded finding f-021.
[tool] d-06 post_finding {"claim": "BBN robustness check. I used linearized f-009 slopes (dYp/dN_eff = 0.0135, dD/H/dN_eff = 0.331e-5) about the server SM values (Yp = 0.246815, D/H = 2
[finding] f-022 BBN robustness check. I used linearized f-009 slopes (dYp/dN_eff = 0.0135, dD/H/dN_eff = 0.331e-5) about the server SM values (Yp = 0.246815, D/H = 2.4433e-5). 
**Finding:** BBN robustness check. I used linearized f-009 slopes (dYp/dN_eff = 0.0135, dD/H/dN_eff = 0.331e-5) about the server SM values (Yp = 0.246815, D/H = 2.4433e-5). The 95% upper bounds on Delta N_eff are given as Delta chi^2 = 3.84 against the SM, with Delta chi^2 = 3.84 against the best fit in brackets. Brief data (Yp 0.245 +/- 0.003, D/H 2.53 +/- 0.03): Yp-only 0.32; D/H-only 0.58 [0.44]; combined 0.47 [0.37]. Server data (Yp 0.2449 +/- 0.004, D/H 2.547 +/- 0.025): Yp-only 0.46; D/H-only 0.66 [0.46]; combined 0.61 [0.43]. The server tool reports 0.548. D/H alone prefers Delta N_eff = 0.26 (brief data) or 0.31 (server data) because the SM D/H sits 2.9 or 4.1 sigma low, so the bound depends on the baseline at the 0.1-0.2 level. Joint bound with dYp/dxi_e = -0.25, assuming D/H does not depend on xi_e, using 2-dof Delta chi^2 = 5.99. Brief data: Delta N_eff in [0.04, 0.48] and xi_e = 0.0073 + 0.054 Delta N_eff +/- 0.029. Server data: Delta N_eff in [0.13, 0.50] and xi_e = 0.0077 + 0.054 Delta N_eff +/- 0.039. So |xi_e| <~ 0.03-0.06 overall, and a positive xi_e of about 0.03 compensates Delta N_eff of about 0.5 in Yp.
